**When to use**
- Simple equality, range, sorting, pagination
- Query predicates are known at compile time
- Method name stays readable

**Entity mapping**

```java
@Document(collection = "products")
public class Product {
  @Id String id;
  @Indexed String skuCode;
  String category;
  String status;
  double price;
  Instant createdAt;
}
```

**Repository**

```java
public interface ProductRepository extends MongoRepository<Product, String> {

  List<Product> findByCategoryAndStatus(String category, String status);

  List<Product> findByPriceBetween(double min, double max);

  boolean existsBySkuCode(String skuCode);

  Page<Product> findByCategory(String category, Pageable pageable);

  // Projection interface — return only a subset of fields
  List<ProductSummary> findByStatus(String status);
}

public interface ProductSummary {
  String getId();
  String getSkuCode();
  double getPrice();
}
```

**When to use**
- Query uses operators derived methods can't express (`$or`, `$elemMatch`, `$regex`)
- Nested field paths or array filters
- Method name would otherwise be 100 characters

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends MongoRepository<Order, String> {

  @Query("{ 'customerId': ?0, 'status': { $in: ?1 } }")
  List<Order> findByCustomerAndStatuses(String customerId, List<String> statuses);

  @Query("{ 'items': { $elemMatch: { 'sku': ?0, 'qty': { $gt: ?1 } } } }")
  List<Order> findOrdersContainingSkuOver(String sku, int qty);

  // Projection via the `fields` attribute
  @Query(value = "{ 'customerId': ?0 }",
         fields = "{ 'status': 1, 'totalCents': 1 }")
  List<Order> findSummariesByCustomer(String customerId);

  // Sort by a field the derived parser can't reach
  @Query(value = "{ 'status': ?0 }", sort = "{ 'createdAt': -1 }")
  List<Order> findLatestByStatus(String status);
}
```

**Parameters are positional** (`?0`, `?1`) and substituted into the JSON fragment.

**When to use**
- Predicates depend on runtime input (search forms, feature flags)
- You want to skip a predicate when its parameter is null
- You need `explain`, hints, or collation control

```java
@Service
public class ProductSearchService {

  private final MongoTemplate mongoTemplate;

  public ProductSearchService(MongoTemplate mongoTemplate) {
    this.mongoTemplate = mongoTemplate;
  }

  public Page<Product> search(ProductFilter f, Pageable pageable) {
    List<Criteria> and = new ArrayList<>();
    if (f.category() != null) and.add(Criteria.where("category").is(f.category()));
    if (f.status()   != null) and.add(Criteria.where("status").is(f.status()));
    if (f.minPrice() != null) and.add(Criteria.where("price").gte(f.minPrice()));
    if (f.maxPrice() != null) and.add(Criteria.where("price").lte(f.maxPrice()));

    Criteria criteria = and.isEmpty()
        ? new Criteria()
        : new Criteria().andOperator(and.toArray(new Criteria[0]));

    Query query = new Query(criteria).with(pageable);
    query.fields().include("skuCode").include("price").include("status");

    long total = mongoTemplate.count(Query.of(query).limit(-1).skip(-1), Product.class);
    List<Product> rows = mongoTemplate.find(query, Product.class);
    return new PageImpl<>(rows, pageable, total);
  }
}
```

**When to use**
- `$group`, `$lookup`, `$unwind`, `$facet`, window functions
- Reports and derived views that repositories can't express

```java
public OrderTotals totalsForCustomer(String customerId, Instant since) {

  MatchOperation  match   = Aggregation.match(
      Criteria.where("customerId").is(customerId)
              .and("createdAt").gte(since));

  GroupOperation  group   = Aggregation.group("customerId")
      .count().as("orderCount")
      .sum("totalCents").as("totalCents");

  LookupOperation lookup  = Aggregation.lookup("customers", "_id", "_id", "customer");

  ProjectionOperation project = Aggregation.project("orderCount", "totalCents")
      .and("customer.name").as("customerName");

  Aggregation agg = Aggregation.newAggregation(match, group, lookup, project);

  return mongoTemplate.aggregate(agg, "orders", OrderTotals.class)
                      .getUniqueMappedResult();
}
```

**`ReactiveMongoRepository` + `ReactiveMongoTemplate`**

```java
public interface ReactiveProductRepository
    extends ReactiveMongoRepository<Product, String> {

  Flux<Product> findByCategory(String category);

  @Query("{ 'price': { $lt: ?0 } }")
  Flux<Product> findCheaperThan(double maxPrice);

  Mono<Long> countByStatus(String status);
}

@Service
public class ReactiveProductService {
  private final ReactiveMongoTemplate rt;
  public ReactiveProductService(ReactiveMongoTemplate rt) { this.rt = rt; }

  public Flux<Product> search(String category) {
    Query q = new Query(Criteria.where("category").is(category));
    return rt.find(q, Product.class);
  }
}
```

**Transactions** require `MongoTransactionManager` (blocking) or `ReactiveMongoTransactionManager` (reactive) and a replica set — single-node `mongod` cannot run multi-document transactions.

**Define the listener**

```java
@Component
public class OrderChangeListener
    implements MessageListener<ChangeStreamDocument<Document>, Order> {

  @Override
  public void onMessage(Message<ChangeStreamDocument<Document>, Order> msg) {
    ChangeStreamDocument<Document> raw = msg.getRaw();
    Order order = msg.getBody();
    OperationType op = raw.getOperationType();
    switch (op) {
      case INSERT, REPLACE, UPDATE -> cache.put(order.getId(), order);
      case DELETE                  -> cache.evict(raw.getDocumentKey().getString("_id").getValue());
      default                      -> { /* drop/rename/invalidate */ }
    }
  }
}
```

**Wire the container**

```java
@Configuration
public class ChangeStreamConfig {

  @Bean(destroyMethod = "stop")
  MessageListenerContainer container(MongoTemplate mongoTemplate,
                                     OrderChangeListener listener) {
    MessageListenerContainer container =
        new DefaultMessageListenerContainer(mongoTemplate);

    ChangeStreamRequestOptions options = new ChangeStreamRequestOptions(
        null, "orders",
        ChangeStreamOptions.builder()
            .fullDocumentLookup(FullDocument.UPDATE_LOOKUP)
            .filter(Aggregation.newAggregation(
                Aggregation.match(Criteria.where("operationType").in("insert", "update", "replace", "delete"))))
            .build());

    container.register(new ChangeStreamRequest<>(listener, options), Order.class);
    container.start();
    return container;
  }
}
```

**`UPDATE_LOOKUP`** rehydrates the post-change document on update events; without it, update events carry only the changed fields.

```java
@Service
public class ReactiveOrderEventService {

  private final ReactiveMongoTemplate rt;
  private final ResumeTokenStore tokens;
  private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

  public ReactiveOrderEventService(ReactiveMongoTemplate rt,
                                   ResumeTokenStore tokens,
                                   ApplicationEventPublisher events) {
    this.rt = rt; this.tokens = tokens; this.events = events;
  }

  @EventListener(ApplicationStartedEvent.class)
  public void start() {
    BsonDocument lastToken = tokens.load("orders");

    ChangeStreamOptions.ChangeStreamOptionsBuilder opts =
        ChangeStreamOptions.builder()
            .fullDocumentLookup(FullDocument.UPDATE_LOOKUP)
            .filter(Aggregation.newAggregation(
                Aggregation.match(Criteria.where("operationType").in("insert", "update", "replace", "delete"))));
    if (lastToken != null) opts.resumeAt(lastToken);

    rt.changeStream("orders", opts.build(), Order.class)
      .doOnNext(event -> {
        Order order = event.getBody();
        events.publishEvent(new OrderChanged(event.getRaw().getOperationType(), order));
        BsonDocument tok = event.getRaw().getResumeToken();
        if (tok != null) tokens.save("orders", tok);
      })
      .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(10, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
      .subscribe();
  }
}
```

**Resume-token persistence** (Redis, Mongo collection, or Postgres) is what makes this an at-least-once consumer — combine with idempotent downstream handlers for effective-once semantics.
